  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to use a combination of structured initialization and stochastic optimization to find a better C5 bound. The key bottleneck is poor convergence in local optimization methods due to suboptimal initializations. By using diverse initial patterns and leveraging convexity in parts of the problem, we aim to escape local optima and find a better solution. This approach prioritizes structured initialization over random patterns, ensuring both feasibility and diversity in the search space.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\n- h \u2208 \u211d^{n_points} (continuous variables for h[i])\n- t \u2208 \u211d (new variable to represent the maximum overlap)\n\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i (enforces h in [0,1])\n- sum(h) = n_points / 2 (exact integral constraint)\n- For each lag k, the overlap integral is approximated as a weighted sum over discrete indices. This is linearized under the assumption that h is piecewise constant over intervals of length dx = 2/n_points.\n\nObjective:\n- Minimize t, where t is the maximum overlap integral over all lags.\n\nDerivation:\nThe overlap integral for lag k is approximated as a weighted sum of h[i] * (1 - h[j]) for overlapping indices i and j. This is linearized by precomputing all pairwise products and forming constraints that each lag's integral does not exceed t. \n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInputs:\n- n_points (number of samples)\n- initial_h_values (optional starting point)\n\nSteps:\na. Check for provided initial_h_values and resample if necessary. If unavailable, construct a structured binary pattern:\n   - Create a binary array where exactly n_points/2 entries are 1s.\n   - Distribute the 1s evenly by placing them every (n_points//m) steps, with m = n_points/2.\n   - If the spacing is uneven, allocate remaining 1s proportionally to maintain even distribution.\n   - Normalize the initial guess to ensure sum(h) = n_points/2.\n\nb. Define the objective function as the maximum value of the overlap integral (using np.correlate with 'full' mode) scaled by dx.\n\nc. Use SLSQP optimization with:\n   - Bounds: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n   - Constraint: sum(h) = n_points / 2\n   - Iterations: 500\n   - Tolerance: ftol=1e-8\n\nd. During optimization:\n   - Clip values to [0,1] if they exceed bounds\n   - Rescale h to maintain the sum constraint\n   - Precompute convolution for validation\n\ne. Post-optimization:\n   - Validate that all h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n   - Recheck sum(h) \u2248 n_points/2 within 1e-6\n   - Recompute C5 using the original evaluator\n   - Store the best solution found\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault settings:\n- n_points = 320 for better resolution\n- Use structured binary initialization for even distribution\n- If initial_h_values provided, resample to match n_points using linear interpolation\n\nVariations:\n- If no parent provided, start with a random binary pattern of evenly spaced 1s and 0s\n- For faster computation, use smaller n_points (e.g., 256) with careful scaling\n- Run multiple optimizations with different parameter settings:\n   - Vary the number of iterations (250-750)\n   - Change the ftol (1e-8 to 1e-10)\n   - Adjust the initial pattern by shifting or scrambling the 1s\n- If solver fails, revert to structured initialization and re-solve\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime estimation:\n- Precompute overhead: negligible\n- Optimization execution: ~O(n_points^2) in time complexity\n- 320 points: ~100k operations, well within 600-second budget\n\nSafety measures:\n- Track best solution found during iterations\n- On solver failure, fallback to structured initialization and re-solve\n- If no improvement for 30 seconds, terminate and return best found\n- If constraints are violated, apply a repair step by projecting to the feasible set\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nPost-solution checks:\n- Ensure all h[i] \u2208 [0,1] with absolute tolerance < 1e-6\n- Verify sum(h) \u2248 n_points / 2 with absolute error < 1e-6\n- Recalculate C5 with original evaluator to match exact metric\n- Check that maximum lag value exceeds all other lags\n- Ensure returned h is a NumPy array of correct shape and type\n\nSanity checks:\n- Verify no division by zero or invalid indices\n- Ensure the maximum overlap integral is correctly computed\n- Validate that the initial and final solutions are compatible\n- If a parent is provided, ensure the final solution is a refined version of it",
